<section id="Pt0.A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Qualitative Analysis</h2>

<div id="Pt0.A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct qualitative analysis on the PRW dataset and compare HAMW with SeqNet, PSTR, and COAT. As shown in Fig.<a href="#Pt0.A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Appendix A Qualitative Analysis ‣ Transformer-Based Person Search with High-Frequency Augmentation and Multi-Wave Mixing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and Fig.<a href="#Pt0.A1.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix A Qualitative Analysis ‣ Transformer-Based Person Search with High-Frequency Augmentation and Multi-Wave Mixing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, other methods are prone to misidentifications when persons have similar colors or are too small, suggesting that they fail to adequately capture high-frequency details and thus struggle to extract discriminative features. In contrast, our model HAMW, trained with high-frequency enhanced features, can more accurately detect subtle cues on persons and avoid such errors.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Pt0.A1.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.23202v1/fig9.png" id="Pt0.A1.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/229;" width="476" height="229" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Top-1 person search qualitative results of THAWM, SeqNet, PSTR, and COAT on the CUHK-SYSU dataset. Yellow, green, and red boxes indicate the query, correct matches, and incorrect matches, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="Pt0.A1.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.23202v1/fig10.png" id="Pt0.A1.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/178;" width="476" height="178" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Top-1 person search qualitative results of THAWM, SeqNet, PSTR, and COAT on the PRW dataset. Yellow, green, and red boxes indicate the query, correct matches, and incorrect matches, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Hyperparameter Analysis</h2>

<div id="Pt0.A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ratio of High-Frequency Tokens <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>: The parameter <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> controls the ratio of tokens selected for computing the high-frequency augmentation loss. The number of tokens in the feature map multiplied by <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> gives the value of <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> mentioned earlier. It reflects the assumed ratio of high-frequency information within the entire feature map. As shown in Fig.<a href="#Pt0.A2.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Appendix B Hyperparameter Analysis ‣ Transformer-Based Person Search with High-Frequency Augmentation and Multi-Wave Mixing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>(a), the model achieves the best performance when <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>=0.3. A larger <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> may mistakenly include some low-frequency components, which could degrade the performance.
<br class="ltx_break">Momentum Coefficient <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>: The coefficient <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is used for updating the Table <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> during the computation of the high-frequency augmentation loss. It balances the importance between new and old information: smaller <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> values make the model more sensitive and adaptive, while larger values lead to more stable and noise-resistant behavior. As shown in Fig.<a href="#Pt0.A2.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Appendix B Hyperparameter Analysis ‣ Transformer-Based Person Search with High-Frequency Augmentation and Multi-Wave Mixing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>(b), both <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>=0.5 and <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>=0.55 yield comparable and optimal results.
<br class="ltx_break">Quantization Interval <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>: The parameter <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> controls the degree of quantization applied to the low-frequency subbands. A larger <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> leads to more aggressive suppression of low-frequency components. While removing low-frequency information can be beneficial, it is also crucial to retain meaningful low-frequency cues. As shown in Fig.<a href="#Pt0.A2.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Appendix B Hyperparameter Analysis ‣ Transformer-Based Person Search with High-Frequency Augmentation and Multi-Wave Mixing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>(c) indicate that the model achieves the highest accuracy when <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>=15. Larger values of <math id="Pt0.A2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> may start to harm the model’s performance.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<figure id="Pt0.A2.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23202v1/Figure_3.svg" id="Pt0.A2.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/140;" width="476" height="140"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>Model performance under different hyperparameter settings on CUHK-SYSU and PRW datasets.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="Pt0.A2.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Efficiency Comparison</h4>

<div id="Pt0.A2.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A2.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate the efficiency of HAMW, we report the inference time (in milliseconds) on a Tesla V100 GPU. For fair comparison, all input images are resized to 900 × 1500. As shown in Table <a href="#Pt0.A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Efficiency Comparison ‣ Appendix B Hyperparameter Analysis ‣ Transformer-Based Person Search with High-Frequency Augmentation and Multi-Wave Mixing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, HAMW achieves the fastest inference speed. This improvement is mainly attributed to replacing the self-attention mechanism with the proposed multi-wave mixing layers.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Pt0.A2.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>The inference speed of different methods on the PRW dataset is summarized to compare their computational efficiency.
</figcaption>
